1.	What is Bash Scripting
________________________________________
1. What is Shell and Shell Scripting?
Shell
•	A shell is a command-line interface (CLI) in Linux/Unix that allows the user to interact with the operating system.
•	It acts as a bridge between the user and the kernel.
•	Users type commands → Shell interprets commands → Kernel executes them.
Shell Scripting
•	A shell script is a text file containing a sequence of shell commands.
•	When executed, the shell runs these commands automatically, just like programming.
•	Used for:
o	Automation (backups, installations)
o	System administration
o	Repetitive tasks
o	Monitoring
Example:
echo "Hello World"
________________________________________
2. Architecture of Shell in Operating System
Basic Architecture Flow
User → Shell → Kernel → Hardware
Explanation:
1.	User types a command.
2.	Shell interprets the command.
3.	Shell sends system calls to the Kernel.
4.	Kernel interacts with hardware (CPU, RAM, Disk).
5.	Results return to the shell and shown to the user.
This architecture ensures user-friendly interaction with complex internal system components.

Architecture of Shell in an Operating System (Diagram)
 
________________________________________
3. Types of Shells
Linux/Unix offers multiple shells, each with different features:
Shell	Full Form	Executable	Key Features
sh	Bourne Shell	/bin/sh	Oldest shell, very stable
bash	Bourne Again Shell	/bin/bash	Most popular; enhanced version of sh
ksh	Korn Shell	/bin/ksh	Faster, supports arrays and scripting
zsh	Z Shell	/bin/zsh	Highly customizable, used in macOS
csh	C Shell	/bin/csh	Syntax similar to C language
Popular Choice for Scripting: bash
•	Default shell in most Linux systems
•	User-friendly
•	Supports functions, arrays, loops
________________________________________
4. What is Bash? (Bourne Again Shell)
•	Bash = Bourne Again Shell
•	It is an improved version of the original Bourne Shell (sh).
•	Default shell in:
o	Red Hat/CentOS/Fedora
o	Ubuntu (until recently)
o	Many Linux distros
Features of Bash:
•	Command history
•	Aliases
•	Tab completion
•	Loops, functions, arrays in scripts
•	Error handling
•	Better usability than sh
________________________________________
5. Shell vs Terminal vs Command Line
Term	Meaning
Shell	The program that interprets commands (e.g., bash).
Terminal	The software window that displays the shell (e.g., GNOME Terminal, xterm).
Command Line	The actual interface where you type commands (prompt area).
Simple Example:
•	Terminal is the room
•	Shell is the person in the room taking orders
•	Command Line is the notebook where you write the orders
________________________________________
6. Writing and Executing a Basic Bash Script
Step 1: Create a file
#vim  myscript.sh
Step 2: Add shebang and commands
#!/bin/bash
echo "Hello, this is my first script!"
Shebang (#!)
•	Located at the top of script
•	Tells the system which shell should execute the script
•	Example:
o	#!/bin/bash
o	#!/bin/sh
o	#!/usr/bin/zsh
________________________________________
7. File Permissions & Making Scripts Executable
Check permissions:
ls -l myscript.sh
Example output:
-rw-r--r-- 1 user user 25 Jan 1 10:00 myscript.sh
Give execute permission:
chmod +x myscript.sh
Run the script:
./myscript.sh
Permission Types:
•	r = read
•	w = write
•	x = execute
________________________________________
8. Comments in Shell Scripts
Comments are ignored by the shell.
Single-line comment:
# This is a comment
Using comments for documentation:
#!/bin/bash

# This script prints system time
echo "Current time is:"
date
Why comments are important:
•	Help others understand your script
•	Make maintenance easier
•	Improve code readability

		
		
		
		
		
		
		

